⍝ Numeral literals: `3` is `Suc (Suc (Suc Zero))`, the same term.  A
⍝ literal is a numeral of the type its context gives it; here nothing
⍝ else is declared `Zero | Suc t`, so every literal is a `nat`.  A
⍝ literal is unary: `n` is 8n + 1 atoms.  `toNum` is the case of
⍝ words-to-numbers, written with literals, and compiles to its term.
nat ≡ Zero | Suc nat
word ≡ One | Two | Three
bool ≡ PTrue | PFalse

toNum w = w ▹ { One 1; Two 2; Three 3 }
three ≔ toNum Three
answer ≔ natAdd (toNum Two) 40
isSix n = natIfEq n 6 PTrue PFalse
pred n = n ▹ { Zero 0; Suc m m }
five ≔ pred 6
